Real fullscreen, back to the state before it, and a white frame background.

Fullscreen covered the work area only: fullzoom() measured it against
BorderedLayout, the screen less the strips panels reserve with
_NET_WM_STRUT, so the taskbar stayed visible under a fullscreen video.  It
is measured against the whole monitor now, and the focused fullscreen
window already stacks above docks, so it covers everything.

Leaving fullscreen went back to the geometry saved before any zoom: a
maximised window put into fullscreen came back at the smaller size it had
before it was maximised, and no longer maximised.  The zoom the window had
when it went fullscreen and that zoom's geometry are now kept, and leaving
fullscreen restores both; the geometry saved before the first zoom stays the
one a later restore goes back to.  Going from fullscreen straight into
another zoom also drops the fullscreen stacking now (unzoom() missed the
F_XFULLSCREENZOOM case).

New Color keyword FrameBackground: the colour a frame shows where the
application has not drawn yet -- the strip a resize uncovers while the
program is busy, or a window just taken over when ctwm restarts.  GTK
windows have no X background of their own, so that was the border tile
grey.  Unset, the border tile colour is used as before.

